Hydrogen produced by steam methane reforming (SMR), without purification, is essentially useless for refineries.

This is because the product of SMR, called “Syngas”, is a mixture of hydrogen, carbon monoxide\*, carbon dioxide, and small quantities of other contaminants.

Hydrogen is used by refineries primarily in hydrotreating, isomerization, and hydrocracking. Each process uses catalyst.

Carbon oxides are extremely efficient catalyst poisons. Most impressively, COx (carbon oxides) concentration of ~20 PPMv can cause accelerated poison for some catalysts. [1]

Pressure Swing Adsorption (PSA) takes advantage of the size of the hydrogen molecule (smallest in the universe) to purify syngas.

For industrial PSA applications, different adsorbents are layered within multiple “beds”. Each adsorbent is designed to remove one or more impurities.

Hydrogen purities of >99.9% are typical for PSA. [2]

\*After steam methane reforming, most hydrogen production facilities utilize either High Temperature (HT) or Low Temperature (LT) water-gas shift reactions upstream of the PSA to convert CO to CO2 and increase hydrogen production.

